## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical Azure storage patterns, including idempotency design, per-tenant isolation, and integration with core Azure services—essential skills for building enterprise-ready serverless applications.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Functions HTTP trigger for API endpoints
- Azure Table Storage partitioning strategy using tenantId as PartitionKey and fileName as RowKey for efficient, tenant-isolated queries
- ADLS Gen2 Blob Storage for scalable file persistence
- Idempotent design patterns using conditional inserts or upserts in Table Storage
- Retriable operations through transactional writes or try-catch with appropriate retry logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: HTTP-triggered function
module.exports = async function(context, req) {
  const { tenantId, fileName, contentBase64 } = req.body;
  const tableClient = /* Table Storage client */;
  const blobClient = /* ADLS Gen2 blob client */;

  const existing = await tableClient.getEntity(tenantId, fileName);
  if (existing) {
    return { status: 200, body: { message: 'File already uploaded', ...existing } };
  }

  const content = Buffer.from(contentBase64, 'base64');
  await blobClient.upload(content);
  await tableClient.upsert({ partitionKey: tenantId, rowKey: fileName, ... });

  return { status: 201, body: { message: 'File uploaded successfully' } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle large files (streaming) to avoid memory pressure?
- How would you test idempotency guarantees with concurrent uploads?
- How would you implement retry logic for transient failures?
- What security considerations apply to per-tenant file access?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design lightweight auditing with low latency using Append Blobs and JWT parsing; demonstrates immutability, data-audit separation, and basic error handling.

## Key Concepts
- Append Blob usage for immutable, append-only logs
- JWT payload extraction from Authorization header
- Per-event audit entries with timestamp and eventId
- Resilient writes with basic retry/backoff

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Azure Function snippet (TypeScript)
import { ContainerClient, AppendBlobClient } from "@azure/storage-blob";
const containerClient = new ContainerClient("<storage-url>", new DefaultAzureCredential());
export async function run(context, req){
  const auth = req.headers["authorization"] ?? "";
  const token = auth.split(" ")[1] ?? "";
  const payload = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(token.split(".")[1], 'base64').toString());
  const userId = payload?.sub ?? "anonymous";
  const eventId = req.body?.eventId ?? crypto.randomUUID();
  const line = JSON.stringify({ ts: new Date().toISOString(), eventId, userId, status: 'received' }) + "\n";
  const audit = containerClient.getAppendBlobClient("audit.log");
  await audit.appendBlock(line, line.length);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: { eventId } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle audit log rotation or retention?
- How would you enforce per-tenant isolation for audits?

